::PC_U04_L04_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Which formula matches cos(A+B)?
{
~A. cos(A)+cos(B)#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
=B. cos(A)cos(B)-sin(A)sin(B)#Correct. The cosine sum formula is cos(A)cos(B)-sin(A)sin(B).
~C. sin(A)cos(B)+cos(A)sin(B)#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
~D. tan(A)+tan(B)#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
}
::PC_U04_L04_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Which formula matches sin(2x)?
{
~A. sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
~B. cos^2(x)-sin^2(x)#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
~C. sin(x)+cos(x)#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
=D. 2sin(x)cos(x)#Correct. The sine double-angle formula is sin(2x)=2sin(x)cos(x).
}
::PC_U04_L04_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
What should be checked when using a half-angle formula?
{
=A. The sign based on the half-angle quadrant#Correct. The quadrant determines the sign of the square-root result.
~B. Only the numerator#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
~C. Only the denominator#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
~D. Whether sine equals cosine always#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
}
::PC_U04_L04_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
What is cos(2x) if cos(x)=4/5 and sin(x)=3/5?
{
~A. 7/5#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
~B. 24/25#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
=C. 7/25#Correct. cos(2x)=cos^2(x)-sin^2(x)=16/25-9/25=7/25.
~D. 1#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
}
::PC_U04_L04_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Why is sin(A+B)=sin(A)+sin(B) incorrect?
{
~A. Because sine is never defined#This choice does not match the required identity or formula.
~B. Because angles cannot be added#This reflects a common misconception; write the identity before substituting.
=C. Because trigonometric angle formulas have specific product terms#Correct. The sine sum formula uses products of sine and cosine terms.
~D. Because sine only works for 90 degrees#This answer does not preserve the trigonometric relationship from the lesson.
}
